  "worked_solution": "First, notice that $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}\\,{e}^{x/y}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{(2x+y){e}^{x/y}}{2y\\sqrt{x}}$ is defined only when $x>0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{y}(x,y)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}{e}^{x/y}}{{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
